# EXPECT: assigns enclosing name
#
# A nested `define` that only WRITES an enclosing name and never reads it.
#
# THE POINT OF THIS FIXTURE is that the outward-mutation guard could not fire on
# its own headline case. Both throws in the lift pass sat behind
# `uses_ident of [fbody, cand]`, and `uses_ident` matches only ["ident", name] —
# but an assignment target is a BARE STRING at node[1], never an ident node. A
# write-only nested define therefore looked like it did not mention the name at
# all:
#
#   VM   7        AOT  100        both build, both rc 0, no diagnostic
#
# The write landed in a fresh C local inside the lifted function, because the
# name never entered the rename map either.
#
# The isolating experiment is one line: add any READ of `x` to the same define
# and it refuses correctly. A guard whose reachability test cannot see the
# construct it guards against is not a guard; it is a comment with a throw
# attached.
#
# This also swallowed a capture shape: two nested defines where the second
# writes what the first captured (VM 8, AOT 101, both rc 0).

define f(y) as:
    local x is 100
    define b(d) as:
        x is 7
        return d
    local t is b of 1
    return x

print of (f of 0)
